verdict.answer:
A Member State may exempt these vehicles from the rules on driving times, breaks and rest periods (Articles 5 to 9 of Regulation 561/2006). It may do so only if the objectives of Article 1 are not prejudiced, and the exemption applies on its own territory, or on another Member State's territory if that State agrees. The Member State may attach individual conditions. The exemption covers a vehicle or combination only if all of these apply:
- its maximum permissible mass does not exceed 7,5 tonnes;
- it is used by a universal service provider (as defined in the Postal Directive 97/67/EC) to deliver items as part of the universal service;
- it is used only within a 100 km radius of the undertaking's base;
- driving is not the driver's main activity.
Member States may also exempt these vehicles from the tachograph rules of Regulation 165/2014. Note that Clarification note 3 (2007) predates the 2020 changes and describes a different, older wording of Article 13(1)(d) (50 km radius, carrying material or equipment), so follow the current Regulation.
